WEBVTT

NOTE Automatically generated captions. Technical spellings normalized.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
stages of our project, we wanted to investigate the trade-offs between learning-based and traditional

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:11.760
cloth simulation, especially for robotic manipulation. We're specifically interested in things like

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.880
real-to-sim transfer from real data, and also the speed at which the cloth can be simulated.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:24.480
On the left, we have a method that we implemented called extended position-based dynamics.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:32.480
In the second column, we have a simple mass spring model, and here you can see some visual results.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:40.000
On the right is a method called physics-guided residual dynamics, which is a learning-based method,

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:44.000
and it is distilled from the extended position-based dynamics teacher.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:52.880
We also investigated some more complex simulation, so here on the right is a

00:00:52.880 --> 00:01:01.840
MuJoCo-based simulator, and on the left and in the middle column are open-loop rollouts from

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:07.520
a method called 3D World Simulator, and here you can see that there are some artifacts,

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.040
but it is doing a pretty good job of simulating, and for the next steps, we want to investigate

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:19.680
speed trade-offs between the neural-based methods and the traditional simulation-based methods,

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.920
and also our traditional simulation-based methods that we implemented don't have collisions or

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:27.400
robot arms, so we will continue to investigate that.

